es to y=124, moves right ~1.5*24=36 px while rising, so at apex he's at ~286 and y=124, above the Goomba (Goomba at 280). Good, clears it. He continues right, lands at ~frame 40 at level ~310. Then I press A again at frame ~44 to jump from 310, hitting the ? block at 310-325. \n\nThen release and wait for the mushroom, which pops out of the block, lands on top, slides right. Mario is at ~level 330 standing on ground; mushroom slides right at ~0.75px/f from ~310, reaching him in ~30 frames.\n\nBut careful: the second ? block (coin) is at ~340-355; jumping from 310 rising might hit the first ? block (mushroom) at 310-325. Good.\n\nLet me do: [{right+a,24},{right,16},{right+a,20},{right,4},{},150]\nTotal 214 frames.\n\nWatch $0756, $0754, $0755, $0757, $079F, $07A0, $075E, $075A, $00CE, $0086.\n\nLet's go. Actually before that, snapshot s_small2 (small Mario before mushroom). Let me take snapshot first.\n\nActually I already have s_small from earlier at the same state (level start). But Mario is now at a different x. For diff purposes, position/other changes will flood. Better to compare power state. Let me snapshot right before the mushroom is collected, i.e. after hitting the block but before touching it. Hmm.\n\nAlternative cleaner differential: snapshot (s_pre_mush) now while small; then get mushroom; snapshot (s_post_mush); diff changed. Lots of movers (x, timer, etc.) but the power state should be among them. Then I can narrow by knowing it should go 0->1 (or 1->2? no, small->big might be 0->1).\n\nActually, the known SMB variables: $0754 might be \"PlayerSize\" where 1=small? Hmm. Let's just diff.\n\nActually, better idea: do TWO transitions: small->big (mushroom) and big->small (take damage). That gives a pair of diffs. But damage causes death/knockback.\n\nAlternatively, use fire flower: big->fire changes $0756 to 2. In 1-1 there's no fire flower (only in later levels). Hmm, but we could set world to a level with a fire flower... too much.\n\nActually, here's a cleaner separation: while paused, after getting the mushroom, I can compare to a state where I wrote the power state candidate. Hmm.\n\nLet me just do the straightforward diff.\n\nActually, simplest robust method to identify the exact power-state byte: **watch the candidates during the pickup**. If I watch $0754, $0756, $0757, and a few others every frame, I'll see which one flips exactly when Mario touches the mushroom. That's essentially differential + watch, and very convincing. And I can also snapshot before/after.\n\nEven better: trace_writes write-mode on range $0740-$07FF (192 bytes) during the run — it will list all writers with PCs and values. The power-up routine writing the state will show up.\n\nBut trace_writes and play_input can't run simultaneously? trace_writes installs a callback for N frames and the game must run. It says \"Refused while paused\". So I'd call trace_writes(frames=300) — but that itself runs 300 frames with no input. Hmm, that means I can't provide input during the trace. Unless I set up a state where Mario auto-collects... no.\n\nSo watch via play_input is the way.\n\nLet me do the run with watch on candidates.\n\nLet me pick watch: $0754(1876), $0755(1877), $0756(1878), $0757(1879), $0758(1880), $0759(1881), $075A(1882), $075B(1883), $075C(1884), $075D(1885), $075E(1886), $079F(1951), $07A0(1952), $00CE(206), $0086(134), $0087(135).\n\nThat's 16 addresses. $079F = 1951 decimal. $075E = 1886.\n\nLet me snapshot s_pre_mush first, then run.\n\nHmm wait, careful: the watch samples every 9 frames by default on long runs (530 frames). For a 214-frame run it'd sample every 3 frames maybe. Power state flip should be visible.\n\nAlso I want to time it. Let's do: snapshot s_pre_mush; play_input script with watch; screenshot after.\n\nLet me go."}
