{"type":"text","text":"Done. Both variables are proven and promoted (3 mappings promoted into the ROM's mapping file).\n\n## Power state — `$0756` (0 = small, 1 = big, 2 = fire)\n\n**Encoding:** one byte, 0/1/2.\n\n**Found in the game's own code** (no reference imported): the item power-up routine at CPU `$D80C-$D842` does `LDA $39` (item type) → `$D820 LDA $0756 / BEQ $D840` → `$D840 LDA #$01 / STA $0756` (small→big), or if `$0756==1` → `$D82B LDA #$02 / STA $0756` (big→fire), then `JSR $85F1`, which at `$85FD` tests `$0756==2` to load the fire sprite palette.\n\n**Write proof:** writing `$0756` alone does **not** redraw Mario — the drawn form follows the companion byte `$0754` (the grow/shrink animation at CPU `$B260` does `LDA $0754 / EOR #$01 / STA $0754`). At the World 1-1 start with Mario small, I wrote `$0756`=1 together with `$0754`=0 and stepped 3 frames: `list_sprites` changed from the 16×16 form (2×2, tiles `$3A/$37/$4F` at y=193/201, 4 blank `$FC` fillers) to the 16×32 big form (2×4 grid, tiles `$00/$01/$4C/$4D/$4A/$4B` at y=177/185/193/201), and the screenshot shows tall big Mario. Writing `$0754`=1 returned the 16×16 form.\n\nI also recorded **`$0754` = player_size** (0 = big, nonzero = small) on its own, since that is the byte that makes the size visibly change.\n\n## Star invincibility timer — `$079F`\n\n**Encoding:** one-byte countdown; 0 = off, the Starman sets it to `$23` (35), decrementing ≈1 unit per 20 frames (≈700 frames ≈ 11.7 s).\n\n**Found in code:** CPU `$D816 LDA #$23 / $D818 STA $079F` (Starman pickup, item type 2), read back at `$D7F2 LDA $079F / BNE $D92C`, with the star logic gated on it.\n\n**Write proof:** writing `$079F`=35 made Mario flash — captured frames show him rendered **dark/black** and then **green** while red in the off phase. A 120-frame `play_input` watch read `$079F` = 23, 22, 22, …, 21, …, 17, i.e. the game itself counts the value down. Writing `$079F`=0 stopped the flashing immediately.\n\n## Honest caveat on the method\n\nI ran the find-variable procedure (snapshots, per-frame watches, loads of play_input), but the decisive evidence came from **disassembly plus write-and-observe**, not from a clean differential: I could not make the mushroom block connect reliably (Mario either hit the coin `?` block, got stuck at a pipe, or fell), and a 540-frame run of 1-1 as big Mario with constant jumping never raised `$079F` (no Starman collected — the wiki puts it in the second of two bricks after the 10-coin brick, far into the level; [Super Mario Wiki, World 1-1](https://www.mariowiki.com/World_1-1_(Super_Mario_Bros.))). So the recordings' `source` is `disassembly` and the notes state exactly what was and wasn't shown by play.\n\nSummary: `$0756` = power state (0/1/2), `$0754` = sprite size flag (0=big/1=small), `$079F` = star timer (Starman → 35, ~20 frames/unit). All three are recorded and promoted."}
